  "challenges": [
    {
      "publicId": "chal_coding_pgp_162",
      "name": "keyset-pagination",
      "type": "mini_coding",
      "title": "Page the admin order list with a keyset cursor",
      "description": "The admin order list pages through one restaurant's orders, newest first, with OFFSET: deep pages are slow, and while new orders arrive staff see some orders twice and miss others. The editor holds `admin/order_pages.py` with its key pieces missing, each marked by a `# [ ]` line; some pieces are wrong on purpose. Fill every line so that `fetch_page` orders by `created_at DESC, id DESC`, starts each next page strictly after the last row of the previous one with a row-value comparison, hands the browser an opaque cursor (URL-safe base64 of JSON `{\"created_at\", \"id\"}`), rejects any other cursor with `InvalidCursor(\"invalid cursor\")`, and has an index that serves both queries without a sort. Many orders share one second. Run pages through a restaurant on SQLite while new orders arrive between pages, and reports ids seen twice, ids never shown and the query plans.",
      "difficulty": "medium",
      "outcomes": [
        "query-performance"
      ],
      "points": 30,
      "hints": [
        "Ask what identifies \"where the last page ended\" when three orders share the same second, and which rows the next query must return relative to that point, whatever was inserted in between.",
        "Look at a unique ORDER BY (add the primary key as the last column), a row-value comparison `(a, b) < (:a, :b)`, an index whose columns are the filter column then the ORDER BY columns, and `base64.urlsafe_b64encode` / `binascii.Error`.",
        "Paging a courier's delivery log by `(delivered_at, id)` oldest first: `WHERE courier_id = :c AND (delivered_at, id) > (:delivered_at, :id) ORDER BY delivered_at, id LIMIT :n`, with `CREATE INDEX deliveries_courier_time_id ON deliveries (courier_id, delivered_at, id)`, and a cursor built from the last row's `delivered_at` and `id`."
      ],
